WEBVTT

00:00:00.092 --> 00:00:03.618
Many transactions run at the same time, so

00:00:03.632 --> 00:00:05.684
we need a simple model to reason about

00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:05.947
them.

00:00:06.474 --> 00:00:07.960
Theory gives us that model.

00:00:08.395 --> 00:00:11.026
Real databases then use locks and

00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:13.526
snapshots to get the properties the theory

00:00:13.526 --> 00:00:14.276
describes.

00:00:15.106 --> 00:00:18.067
An operation is one transaction reading or

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.001
writing one datum.

00:00:19.409 --> 00:00:22.053
A schedule is a sequence that mixes the

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.961
operations of several transactions, while

00:00:24.975 --> 00:00:27.396
keeping each transaction's own order.

00:00:27.843 --> 00:00:31.001
Here T1 works on x and T2 works on z.

00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:33.850
With these two small transactions there

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.574
are six possible schedules.

00:00:35.995 --> 00:00:37.074
Two are serial:

00:00:37.535 --> 00:00:40.127
one transaction runs completely, then the

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:40.574
other.

00:00:41.008 --> 00:00:43.706
The other four interleave the operations

00:00:43.785 --> 00:00:44.811
in different ways.

00:00:45.664 --> 00:00:47.625
How many schedules exist in general?

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:50.296
Serial ones are just the orderings of the

00:00:50.296 --> 00:00:51.257
transactions.

00:00:51.678 --> 00:00:54.428
Distinct schedules count all valid mixes

00:00:54.428 --> 00:00:56.572
of operations, which is far bigger.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:00:57.388
Check it:

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:00.506
two transactions with two operations each

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.441
give 4 factorial over 2 times 2, which is

00:01:04.441 --> 00:01:05.059
6.

00:01:05.897 --> 00:01:07.700
The scheduler's goal is to reject

00:01:07.713 --> 00:01:09.674
schedules that cause anomalies.

00:01:10.095 --> 00:01:13.055
A serial schedule is safe but unrealistic.

00:01:13.489 --> 00:01:15.818
So we accept any schedule that leaves the

00:01:15.831 --> 00:01:18.700
database in the same state as some serial

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.239
schedule.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.305
That is called serializable.

00:01:22.135 --> 00:01:25.017
View-serializability makes this precise.

00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:28.438
Two schedules are view-equivalent if every

00:01:28.438 --> 00:01:31.425
read sees the same write and the final

00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:33.688
write on each object is the same.

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:37.425
In S3, two reads on x are swapped compared

00:01:37.425 --> 00:01:40.359
with serial S4, so S3 is

00:01:40.372 --> 00:01:41.530
view-serializable.

00:01:42.357 --> 00:01:43.397
Now a bad case.

00:01:43.818 --> 00:01:47.555
In S7 both transactions read x, then both

00:01:47.568 --> 00:01:48.410
write x.

00:01:48.713 --> 00:01:50.726
Try T1 then T2:

00:01:51.239 --> 00:01:53.989
T2's read would see T1's write, but here

00:01:53.989 --> 00:01:55.620
it sees the original value.

00:01:56.055 --> 00:01:59.726
Try T2 then T1 and T1's read breaks the

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:00.423
same way.

00:02:00.857 --> 00:02:03.660
So S7 is a lost update and is not

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.870
view-serializable.

00:02:05.723 --> 00:02:06.513
There is a catch.

00:02:06.947 --> 00:02:09.197
Deciding whether an arbitrary schedule is

00:02:09.197 --> 00:02:12.368
view-serializable is NP-complete, because

00:02:12.368 --> 00:02:14.486
it means checking many possible serial

00:02:14.486 --> 00:02:15.026
orders.

00:02:15.447 --> 00:02:18.263
So we trade some accuracy for speed, and

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:20.065
look for a stricter definition that is

00:02:20.144 --> 00:02:21.184
easier to check.
